 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Min/Max Tree Construction](1623-min-max-tree-construction.md)
-A min/max tree stored within the octree structure to accelerate ray traversal by skipping cells where the isovalue is outside the node's min/max scalar range. It is computed simultaneously with the bottom-up consolidation. For leaf nodes, min/max is based on 27 voxels (cell + forward neighbors). For interior nodes, min/max is derived from the min/max pairs of the 8 children. This adds ~22% overhead but allows rejection of children without traversal.
+Min/Max Tree Construction builds a hierarchical acceleration structure embedded within an octree to accelerate ray traversal by storing the minimum and maximum scalar values for each node. It solves the problem of inefficient traversal in sparse or large-volume rendering by allowing the renderer to reject entire subtrees when the target isovalue falls outside the node's computed extrema. Unlike sibling methods that focus on structural compression or partitioning, this method specifically optimizes traversal speed by embedding scalar bounds directly into the tree topology, enabling early-out checks during ray casting.
 
 ### [Octree Construction from Structured Volumes](1854-octree-construction-from-structured-volumes.md)
-Generation of an octree from a 3D grid by consolidating voxels with zero inter-voxel variance, extending the principle of quadtree generation from 2D grids by consolidating pixels with zero inter-pixel variance. It uses a 3D array of rectilinear grid data as input and determines $N$ and $d_{max}$ based on the largest dimension.
+This method compresses large structured scalar volumes into an adaptive-resolution octree to reduce memory footprint while serving as an acceleration structure for ray tracing. It solves the problem of storing and rendering large 3D array volumes on commodity machines with limited memory by converting a dense 3D grid into a sparse structure. Unlike Velasco and Torres, who encapsulate eight-voxel cells, this technique encapsulates actual individual voxels, allowing for finer granularity in compression. It extends the principle of quadtree generation from 2D grids (consolidating pixels with zero inter-pixel variance) to 3D octrees by consolidating voxels with zero inter-voxel variance.
+
+### [Hierarchical Partition Optimization](1953-hierarchical-partition-optimization.md)
+Hierarchical Partition Optimization is an offline preprocessing algorithm that constructs an optimal adaptive-resolution hierarchical partition and assigns extraction methods to its nodes, balancing memory consumption against expected extraction time. It solves the problem of finding a partition that minimizes a global Lagrangian cost function under a memory constraint.
 
 ### [Omnitree Refinement](2222-omnitree-refinement.md)
-Omnitrees partition a d-dimensional Cartesian domain $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ into N nonoverlapping subdomains $Q_i$, where boundaries occur at dyadic rationals (multiples of $2^{-k}$). An omnitree node $v$ is characterized by a binary splitting label $b(v) \in \{0, 1\}^d$, where $b_j = 1$ indicates bisection in dimension $j$, yielding $2^{|\sigma(v)|}$ children. The all-zero label $b = 0$ is reserved for leaf nodes. Normalization enforces that splits are gathered as high in the tree as possible, guaranteeing a unique, shallowest representation.
-
-### Hierarchical Partition Optimization
-A method to optimize the octree structure for memory and extraction time trade-offs. It uses a global Lagrangian cost function $J_\lambda(P, A) := E[T(P, A, t)] + \lambda \cdot M(P, A)$, where $E[T]$ is expected extraction time and $M$ is memory [S6, unofficial]. It builds a full partitioning tree $P_{max}$ and runs bottom-up Lagrangian optimization for a sequence of $\lambda_i$ values to find optimal partitions and method assignments for each node [S6, unofficial].
+Omnitree refinement transforms an initial hierarchical dyadic partition of a $d$-dimensional Cartesian domain into a target partition with higher resolution in specific dimensions. It solves the problem of anisotropically increasing the granularity of a sparse voxel representation by attaching refinement markers to nodes and propagating them through the tree structure. The method explicitly supports per-dimension refinement levels ($\vec{m} \in \mathbb{N}^d$), allowing for non-cubic, rectangular subdomains. It builds on [Bottom-up Octree Construction](1590-bottom-up-octree-construction.md) by providing the mechanism to adjust the topology of the resulting tree after initial construction or during dynamic updates.
+
+### [Octree Compaction & Construction](2857-octree-compaction-construction.md)
+This method converts a dynamically allocated, pointer-heavy octree into a dense, cache-friendly contiguous memory layout. It solves the problem of poor spatial locality and high memory overhead associated with traditional pointer-based tree structures by computing relative offsets for children and siblings. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Min/Max Tree Construction](1623-min-max-tree-construction.md) by focusing specifically on the binary encoding of validity/filled masks and the compact storage of child pointers, rather than just hierarchical aggregation.
+
+### [Octree Encoding](5851-octree-encoding.md)
+Spatial domain compression technique where neighboring voxels with similar values are fused into macro voxels.
+
+### DMC Octree Build
+Builds the DMC octree from a scalar field, using a QEF solver and various split policies [S10, unofficial]. It validates that the root size is a power of two, initializes build arguments with a seeded RNG, and collapses the root node before recursively building nodes [S10, unofficial]. The epsilon parameter is scaled according to the QEF scale: $\text{scaled\_epsilon} = \epsilon / (\text{m\_globalScale} \times \text{m\_globalScale})$ [S10, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-
-*   **Time Complexity:** The build process is bottom-up and recursive [S3, S4, unofficial]. Point location is $O(\log(N))$ in the worst case, but neighbor-finding skews heavily toward $O(1)$ in practice [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The structure stores min/max pairs and pointers [S4, unofficial]. The min/max tree adds approximately 22% overhead to the raw scalar octree-compressed data [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Accelerates ray tracing by allowing rejection of children whose min/max range does not contain the isovalue [S3, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Adaptive resolution, lossless/lossy compression, integrated acceleration structure [S1][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Neighbor-finding overhead for cell intersection; potential for looser min/max tree if leaf min/max is omitted (mitigated by runtime computation) [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The bottom-up build algorithm is $O(N)$ with respect to the total number of voxels [S9, unofficial]. It is approximately two times faster than top-down construction because it visits each data value only once [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Space overhead is generally acceptable if large macro voxels are created. Maximum overhead is approximately $v \times b$, where $v$ is the total number of voxels and $b$ is the number of bytes used to store information about each internal tree node [S1, unofficial]. However, building an expanded full octree structure can occupy a footprint of four times the raw volume size, requiring significant memory (e.g., nearly 40 GB for Richtmyer-Meshkov data) [S9, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On a single core of a 16-core 2.4 GHz Opteron workstation, building a single timestep of a $30^3$ heptane volume requires 8 seconds with negligible memory footprint [S9, unofficial]. Building a timestep of Richtmyer-Meshkov data requires 45 minutes [S9, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** High compression ratio for coherent data; fast construction time; supports both lossless and lossy modes; integrates well with ray tracing via min/max bounds [S1, S7, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Lossy compression can degrade image quality for 8-bit data [S1, unofficial]; parallel implementation requires careful synchronization [S2, unofficial]; memory footprint can be high for uncompressed or partially compressed data [S9, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Suitable for large, coherent volume datasets where compression and fast traversal are priorities, and where the data can tolerate some lossiness or is naturally quantized [S1, S9, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for data requiring high-fidelity visualization with small error tolerances (e.g., 8-bit medical data) [S1, unofficial], or when memory is extremely constrained and the volume is highly heterogeneous [S9, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-
-*   **Origin:** The method was introduced in "Interactive Isosurface Ray Tracing of Large Octree Volumes" by Knoll et al. (2006) [S1][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Development:** It extends the principle of quadtree generation from 2D grids to 3D octrees [S2]. It builds on voxel-cell duality concepts and neighbor-finding techniques from prior work [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Usage:** Used in interactive isosurface ray tracing of large scalar fields [S1][S3, unofficial].
+Bottom-up octree construction was developed as part of techniques for efficient encoding and rendering of time-varying volume data [S1, unofficial]. It is closely associated with the work of Wald et al. on interactive isosurface ray tracing of large octree volumes [S4, S5][S6, S7, S9, unofficial]. The method extends the principle of quadtree generation from 2D grids to 3D octrees [S1, S2, unofficial][S4][S6, unofficial][S8]. It is used in volume rendering systems that require adaptive resolution and fast traversal, such as those employing ray casting with empty-space skipping [S3, S7, unofficial].
 
